					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fieldstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1436137); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1436137&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056659" alt="fieldstone" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1436137)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fieldstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1436137); return false;" >fieldstone</a></span> <span class="odate time_1513535590 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Dec 2017 18:33</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3654114">
									

<p>This isn't as far off as you think - modern technology has changed a lot:</p>
<ul>
<li>boiling bottles isn't necessary these days if you have city water</li>
<li>old-fashioned baby formula needed to be warmed so it wouldn't clump, but modern ones will dissolve at any temperature (we make formula with bottled water straight from the fridge)</li>
<li>talcum powder turns out to cause cancer</li>
<li>baby wipes are only needed when cleaning poops, and overusing them causes diaper rash (the way to clean pee is just let the baby air dry for a minute or two before putting the new diaper on)</li>
</ul>
<p>Lots of people still do the extra steps because the older way was modeled for them (and some babies may <em>prefer</em> warm milk).</p>
<p>The part you nailed is that their tiny bellies need a bottle at least every 3 hours, around the clock, for the first 3-4 months (then they can make it without one just long enough to sleep through the night) - new parents getting no sleep that whole time is closer to literal truth than to a joke. Human milk/formula is the only food Munchkin's little system can fit that's dense enough in calories to keep her satisfied (&quot;food for fun until age one&quot;), so unless Kit is giving her a bottle to go with the pudding he's going to have a crying, sleepless mess on his hands :p</p>
<p>This continues to be a nice Christmas treat, but with anticipation building well - like an advent calendar, except a baby might die.</p>

							</div>

								
					
